Mild oral mucosal thermal burn from consuming excessively hot food or beverage
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Immediate pain post-ingestion, Focal mucosal sloughing, Palatal or lingual erythema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-018
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe intraoral pain and mucosal sloughing requiring assessment to exclude chemical ingestion, severe infectious stomatitis, or airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden severe oral pain and peeling skin inside the mouth. Urgent physical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to rule out impending airway edema from an extensive thermal or chemical burn, and to exclude opportunistic oral infections.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I burnt the roof of my mouth on hot pizza and the skin is peeling off; it hurts to drink anything.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes an immediate onset of pain following ingestion of excessively hot food. Reports an exquisitely tender focal area on the hard palate. Notes superficial sloughing of the oral mucosa occurring shortly after the burn. Describes transient localized mild tissue edema. Complete absence of systemic fever, drooling, or difficulty breathing. Denies ingestion of caustic chemicals.
Functional Status: Fully independent, oral fluid intake impaired by pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Dehydration risk if odynophagia prevents adequate fluid intake over subsequent days.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, speaking clearly, sipping water hesitantly.
Physical Exam: Oral examination reveals an exquisitely tender focal area on the hard palate with an erythematous mucosal base and a central white/grayish pseudomembrane of sloughed tissue. Diagnostic triad confirmed: immediate pain post-ingestion, focal mucosal sloughing, and palatal erythema. Intact surrounding unaffected oral mucosa. Normal uvular position without significant edema. Normal dental and periodontal structures. Absence of regional cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Empty pizza box observed in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No history of immunosuppressants or recent antibiotic use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild oral mucosal thermal burn from consuming excessively hot food or beverage - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Oral Candidiasis (Thrush) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute traumatic onset, localized purely to the palate rather than diffuse white plaques that bleed when scraped.

Herpes Simplex Stomatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Immediate temporal relationship to a thermal injury and lack of grouped vesicles or systemic viral prodrome.

Caustic Chemical Ingestion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear history of hot food ingestion, absence of extensive esophageal symptoms, and strictly localized palatal burn.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient suffered an acute intraoral injury resulting in severe odynophagia and visible mucosal sloughing. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically necessary to perform a direct oropharyngeal examination to exclude extension of thermal edema to the uvula or posterior pharynx, which could compromise the airway. The evaluation confirmed a strictly localized, superficial palatal burn without airway involvement, negating the need for emergency department transfer while ensuring adequate oral intake strategies were established.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommendation for viscous lidocaine 2% solution to be swished and spat before meals for acute pain control. Advice on paracetamol/ibuprofen scheduling.
Positive Behavioral Change: Pre-cool hot foods and beverages by allowing them to sit for 5 minutes before the first bite, using the waiting time to prepare utensils, preventing repetitive thermal mucosal trauma.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the lesion does not show signs of healing within 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden difficulty breathing, voice changes, inability to swallow your own saliva, or swelling of the throat.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a superficial thermal injury; the resulting raw surface is prone to minor secondary irritation.
Clinical Course: Spontaneous resolution of acute pain is expected within 24 to 48 hours as the mucosa rapidly regenerates.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you become completely unable to swallow fluids, if the burn site develops foul-smelling pus, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The hot food killed the top layer of cells on the roof of your mouth, exposing the sensitive nerve endings below. The white film is the dead skin sloughing off, serving as a temporary natural bandage.
Actionable Steps: Eat a soft, bland, and cool diet (e.g., yogurt, ice cream) for 48 hours. Avoid acidic, spicy, or sharp foods like chips that can traumatize the raw tissue.

